WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.360
Marcus is offered a new life policy.

2
00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:08.580
He plans to reduce his existing policy to paid-up coverage so

3
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.220
he can afford the new premium.

4
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:14.540
That can be a replacement even though the old policy is

5
00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:20.040
not completely canceled. Maryland's definition includes specified reductions,

6
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.440
surrenders, lapses and other changes caused by buying new coverage.

7
00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:30.540
The test concerns what the proposing producer knows or should know

8
00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:35.580
about the transaction. Start by asking what existing coverage the applicant

9
00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.400
has and exactly how the new purchase will affect it.

10
00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:45.110
Aisha wants to keep her old policy, borrow from its cash

11
00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:49.510
value and use that money to pay the new life policy's

12
00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.990
premium. This is a financed purchase under the replacement definition.

13
00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:57.690
Keeping both policies does not remove the issue.

14
00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:02.810
The borrowing can reduce available values and affect the amount eventually

15
00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:04.730
payable under the old policy.

16
00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:09.750
Compare the continuing costs of both contracts, the loan interest and

17
00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:13.070
the consequences if the plan performs differently than expected.

18
00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:17.930
A producer should not describe borrowed policy money as a free

19
00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:23.040
new benefit. For a covered application, the applicant and producer

20
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.240
sign a statement about existing life insurance or

21
00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.420
annuities. If existing coverage is present, the producer

22
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.580
presents the replacement notice no later than taking the application

23
00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:43.420
and reads it unless the applicant declines that reading.

24
00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:47.500
The notice is signed and left with the applicant.

25
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:52.580
It identifies the affected contracts and whether each is

26
00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.920
replaced or used for financing.

27
00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:01.320
Required sales materials also go to the applicant and the insurer.

28
00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:07.280
Do not wait until delivery to discover an undisclosed replacement.

29
00:02:08.094 --> 00:02:13.314
The replacing insurer verifies the required forms and notifies another

30
00:02:13.314 --> 00:02:18.374
affected existing insurer within five business days after the specified

31
00:02:18.374 --> 00:02:22.854
completed-application receipt or identification of the replacement.

32
00:02:23.534 --> 00:02:28.014
When the existing insurer requests the proposed illustration or summary,

33
00:02:28.534 --> 00:02:32.934
another five-business-day requirement applies to providing the available

34
00:02:32.934 --> 00:02:37.994
document. Registered variable contracts have a specific alternative

35
00:02:37.994 --> 00:02:42.294
involving contributions and prospectus identification.

36
00:02:43.514 --> 00:02:47.734
The producer's paperwork does not replace these insurer duties.

37
00:02:48.694 --> 00:02:53.154
Keeping responsibilities separate helps you answer a question that names the

38
00:02:53.154 --> 00:02:56.074
insurer, the producer or the applicant.

39
00:02:56.808 --> 00:03:00.528
For a covered replacement, the owner receives notice of a right

40
00:03:00.528 --> 00:03:04.608
to return the new contract within thirty days after delivery.

41
00:03:05.328 --> 00:03:09.628
The rule ordinarily provides a full refund of premiums or considerations,

42
00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.028
including fees and charges.

43
00:03:13.248 --> 00:03:18.268
Variable contracts use a different formula: cash surrender value plus the

44
00:03:18.268 --> 00:03:21.388
specified deducted or imposed fees and charges.

45
00:03:22.288 --> 00:03:26.350
Do not promise that variable investment losses disappear.

46
00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:30.648
The chapter also gives credit for elapsed contestability and suicide

47
00:03:30.648 --> 00:03:36.488
periods in specified same-insurer or affiliated-insurer replacements, within

48
00:03:36.488 --> 00:03:37.848
the stated amount limits.

49
00:03:38.208 --> 00:03:39.488
Check the actual transaction.

50
00:03:40.242 --> 00:03:44.782
Marcus should compare both policies before giving up his old coverage.

51
00:03:46.022 --> 00:03:50.122
He needs to understand whether the new coverage is effective, what

52
00:03:50.122 --> 00:03:55.182
guarantees change, which costs restart and how existing

53
00:03:55.182 --> 00:03:56.882
loans affect the result.

54
00:03:57.902 --> 00:04:00.442
Changed health can affect new underwriting.

55
00:04:01.342 --> 00:04:04.982
A replacement may meet a real need, but a sales illustration

56
00:04:04.982 --> 00:04:07.242
alone is not a complete comparison.

57
00:04:07.982 --> 00:04:12.842
Maryland also exempts specified transactions from this chapter,

58
00:04:13.482 --> 00:04:15.742
such as certain contractual conversions.

59
00:04:16.682 --> 00:04:19.202
Identify the scope before applying a deadline.

60
00:04:20.302 --> 00:04:24.202
The professional goal is an informed decision supported by accurate

61
00:04:24.202 --> 00:04:27.222
documents, not replacement for its own sake.
